platform/x86: dell-wmi: ignore type 0x0012 code 0x0000

The XPS 14 DA14260's EC sends an event of type 0x0012 with code
0x0000 about once an hour while the laptop is in use, carrying five
words of extended data (seen: 003c 0008 000d 004b 0001). The keymap
has no entry for it, so each one logs "Unknown key with type 0x0012
and code 0x0000 pressed". Nothing acts on it on Linux, and Dell has
not said what it means, so ignore it rather than report it as a key.

--- a/drivers/platform/x86/dell/dell-wmi-base.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/x86/dell/dell-wmi-base.c
@@ -369,6 +369,12 @@
  * They are events with extended data
  */
 static const struct key_entry dell_wmi_keymap_type_0012[] = {
+	/*
+	 * Sent by the XPS 14 DA14260's EC about hourly while it is in use,
+	 * with five words of extended data whose meaning Dell hasn't given
+	 */
+	{ KE_IGNORE, 0x0000, { KEY_RESERVED } },
+
 	/* Backlight brightness change event */
 	{ KE_IGNORE, 0x0003, { KEY_RESERVED } },
 
